import Foundation

/// The single source for the version the app shows, "What's new" and "How it works".
enum Guide {
    static let appVersion = "6.8"

    struct Release: Identifiable {
        let version: String
        let date: String
        let lines: [String]
        var id: String { version }
    }

    static let releases: [Release] = [
        Release(version: "6.8 (build 11)", date: "26 Nov 2026", lines: [
            "Changed: only words and sample data. The sample jars no longer use real names. The app works as before.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "6.7 (build 10)", date: "26 Nov 2026", lines: [
            "New: pictures on Timeline lines. Before (6.6): a Timeline line was only words; pictures could only belong to the whole jar (Edit jar › Photos). Now: any “What I did” line and any taste test can carry pictures, so you can record the whole process with the camera. Where: on a jar’s page, in the adding area of the Timeline. As before there is the switch What I did | Taste test, “When”, and the text box. New, under the text box: the pictures you picked show as small squares with the word Remove under each. Under them is one row: Add pictures (pick up to 6 from Photos), Take a picture (iPhone and iPad only, where there is a camera) and the full-colour Add button.",
            "Changed: Add works with words, with pictures, or with both. Before: Add stayed off until you had written something. Now: a picture alone is a line too. Example: take a picture of the brine, press Add, and the Timeline gets a line with just that picture.",
            "Changed: several lines at once. Before and now: in What I did, a pasted list still becomes one step for every line (list numbers are removed). New: the pictures go with the last of those new lines. Example: you paste “Chop the cabbage”, “Salt it” and “Pack the jar” and pick 2 pictures: three steps are made and the 2 pictures sit on “Pack the jar”.",
            "Changed: the small grey hint under the adding area. Before (What I did): “One step per line. Paste a whole list and every line becomes its own step — list numbers are removed.” Now: “One step per line — list numbers are removed. Pictures go with the last line; a picture alone is fine too.” Before (Taste test): “A taste test also moves the next taste-test reminder.” Now: “A taste test also moves the next taste-test reminder. A picture alone is fine too.”",
            "New: pictures show on the Timeline. Before: nothing. Now: a line’s pictures show as small squares (60 by 60 points) under its words, still on the rail. A “What I did” line that is only pictures shows the word “Picture” in grey; a taste test that is only pictures shows “Taste” alone. Tap the words and you get “Change this line”, as before; tap a picture and it opens large.",
            "Changed: the note beside the Timeline heading. Before: “10 lines · tap a line to change it”. Now, when at least one line has a picture: “10 lines · tap a line to change it, a picture to see it large”. With no picture on any line it says what it said before.",
            "New: the jar’s photo story. Before (6.3 on): a picture opened large, one jar picture at a time. Now: a Timeline picture opens the story of the whole jar: every Timeline picture of that jar, oldest first (the Timeline order), opened at the picture you tapped. On the iPhone and iPad swipe left and right; on the Mac use Previous and Next or the arrow keys. “3 of 8” stands at the top and Done at the top right (Escape on the Mac).",
            "New: a caption at the bottom of every picture in the story. Examples: “Day 3 · Wed 25 Nov 08:12 — Taste: tangy, still crunchy” and “Day 1 · Mon 23 Nov 10:30 — Packed carrots, brine on top”. A picture-only line has no words part: “Day 1 · Mon 23 Nov 10:30”. A line without a time leaves the time out. The day number counts the start day as Day 1, the same as everywhere else.",
            "New: Change this line (tap a line) has a section Pictures. Before: only the day, the time and the words. Now: the line’s pictures with Remove under each, Add pictures, and Take a picture (iPhone and iPad). Save works when the line has words or pictures. Before: words were needed, so a line could not be left empty.",
            "Changed: the picture on a jar’s head card and the small picture in the Jars list. Before: always the jar’s own first picture (from Edit jar › Photos), else a drawn jar. Now: the newest Timeline picture, that is the last picture of the latest line that has pictures, so the card shows how the jar looks now. When the Timeline has no picture, it is the jar’s own first picture as before; else the drawn jar. Tapping the head card picture opens the photo story at that picture when it came from the Timeline; otherwise it opens the jar’s own pictures as before.",
            "Changed: Share and the spreadsheet. Before: a line was its date, time and words. Now: a line with pictures gets “(1 picture)” or “(2 pictures)” after its words; a line that is only pictures reads just “(1 picture)”. The pictures themselves are not inside the text or the spreadsheet’s “What I did” column. Save steps as a recipe skips lines that are only pictures.",
            "Changed: where pictures live. Each Timeline line stores its own list of picture names. The files go in the same Photos folder as all other pictures, travel through iCloud like them, and are in the safety copies; bringing a safety copy back brings the line pictures back too. Lines you made before 6.7 simply have no pictures.",
            "Good to know: update all three devices. This version changes how the jars are stored (format 1 becomes 2). An iPhone, iPad or Mac that is still on 6.6 shows “Update this device first” and saves nothing until it has 6.7. So update the iPhone, the iPad and the Mac.",
            "Unchanged on purpose: the jar’s own pictures in Edit jar › Photos and the picture row under the head card; the pictures of recipes and bakes; Burped lines; the To do list; the 3-hour rule; and how a taste test moves the next taste-test reminder.",
            "Updated: How it works (Your jars, A jar’s page, Signs in this app) and the written specification.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "6.6 (build 9)", date: "25 Nov 2026", lines: [
            "Changed: all 10 burps sound happier. Before (6.5): low, rough and gruff — deep voices and a lot of growl. Now: higher and brighter, smoother, with less growl; most go up a little at the end, and some end with a small bubble. They are still made for FermentLog from scratch, not recordings, and each still lasts from a quarter of a second to about 1.6 seconds.",
            "Changed: three burps got a new name, because the old one no longer fits the sound. Before: 3 “Deep rumble — Low, from the cellar”, 7 “Gurgle — Wet and wobbly”, 10 “Big one — Loud and rough”. Now: 3 “Chuckle — Burps like a happy laugh”, 7 “Bubbles — Fizzy, like a fresh jar”, 10 “Ta-da — Proud, ends on a high note”. Your switches stay as they were: a burp you switched off stays off, under its new name.",
            "Changed: some lines under the names. Before → now: 1 Classic, “A plain, honest burp” → “A cheerful little burp”; 2 Short pop, “Quick and done” → “Quick and bubbly”; 4 Long one, “Takes its time” → “Takes its time, with a smile”; 6 Double, “Two in a row” → “Two in a row, the second one higher”. The same as before: 5 Squeaky (High and small), 8 Rising (Ends going up, like a question), 9 Tiny (A polite little one).",
            "Changed: the note on the reminder. Before: “Burp: Deep rumble.” (one of the old names). Now: it shows the new names, for example “Burp: Chuckle.”",
            "Unchanged on purpose: which buttons burp, the random pick (never the same one twice in a row), Settings › Burp sounds with Play and a switch for each burp, and the silent switch rule.",
            "Updated: How it works (The burp alarm) and the written specification.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "6.5 (build 8)", date: "25 Nov 2026", lines: [
            "New: every Burped burps. Before (6.0 to 6.4): when you pressed Burped, on a burp reminder or on a jar’s page, the burp was written into the Timeline in silence. Now: both Burped buttons play a burp sound. This works on the iPhone, on the iPad and, when “Ring on this Mac” is on, on the Mac.",
            "New: 10 burps, picked at random. Before: there was no sound at all. Now: each press plays one of the burps that are switched on, picked at random, and never the same one twice in a row (when two or more are on). The ten: Classic (a plain, honest burp), Short pop (quick and done), Deep rumble (low, from the cellar), Long one (takes its time), Squeaky (high and small), Double (two in a row), Gurgle (wet and wobbly), Rising (ends going up, like a question), Tiny (a polite little one) and Big one (loud and rough). They were made for FermentLog from scratch; they are not recordings. Each lasts from a quarter of a second to 2 seconds.",
            "New: Settings › Burp sounds, right under Burp alarm. Before: nothing to choose. Now: a note on top and one row for each burp: a button Play (in the grey-blue of Settings) that plays that burp at once so you can hear it, even with the iPhone on silent; the burp’s name with a small line under it; and a switch on the right (on means it is in the random mix). Under the list: “All off: Burped makes no sound. Your choice is kept on this iPhone; the iPhone, the iPad and the Mac each keep their own.” (on the iPad it says iPad, on the Mac it says Mac). All ten are on to begin with.",
            "New: a small note comes with the burp on a reminder. Before: nothing showed after you pressed Burped. Now: a note says “Burped: Cabbage jar” and under it “Written in the Timeline at 08:12. Burp: Deep rumble.” (the name of the burp that played). It goes away by itself after about 8 seconds. When FermentLog is open on the screen at that moment, only the sound plays and no note shows. With all burps switched off there is no sound and no note.",
            "New: Burped on a jar’s page burps too. Before: that button was always quiet. Now: it plays a random burp from the ones that are on. The bar at the bottom, “Burp logged 08:12” with Undo, is the same as before.",
            "Changed: the note in Settings › Burp alarm. Before: “… The notification has a Burped button — one tap writes the burp into the jar’s Timeline.” Now: “… one tap writes the burp into the jar’s Timeline and plays a burp.”",
            "Good to know: the burps follow your device’s own sound settings. On an iPhone set to silent (the switch on the side) or in a Focus that silences FermentLog there is no burp; for the reminder also when notification sounds for FermentLog are turned off in the system Settings. The iPhone may vibrate instead. Only Play in Settings sounds even on silent.",
            "Unchanged on purpose: the reminder’s own ring (still the normal notification sound), what is written into the Timeline (“Burped the jar” and the time), the 3-hour rule and Undo.",
            "Updated: How it works (The burp alarm, The jar’s page, Settings) and the written specification.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "6.4 (build 7)", date: "25 Nov 2026", lines: [
            "New: a find box on the Jars list. Before (6.3): to find a jar by its name you had to open the Search page. Now: under the three tiles there is a box, “Find a jar by name or vegetable”. Type a few letters and only the jars whose name or vegetable contains them stay in the list, at once, in all three groups. Big or small letters and accents do not matter: “surkal” finds “Surkål”. The word button Clear empties the box. If no jar fits, the list says “No jar matches “xyz”.” The box works together with a tile: choose In the fridge and type “carrot” and you see only the carrot jars in the fridge. The box starts empty every time the list opens. The Search page is still there and still looks everywhere (jars, bakes, recipes and lessons).",
            "New: sort the jars. Before: the jars were always sorted by start date. Now: next to the find box (under it on a narrow iPhone) there is a switch, Sort, with two choices. Start date works as before: in Fermenting the jars due for a taste test come first, the most overdue first, then the newest start; In the fridge and Done show the newest start first. Name puts the jars in A to Z order in every group (big or small letters do not matter, and the letters follow your device’s language, so in Swedish Å, Ä and Ö come after Z); the taste-test order does not apply then. The box “1 jar is due for a taste test” stays on top with either choice. Your choice is remembered on the device you hold: the iPhone, the iPad and the Mac each keep their own.",
            "Unchanged on purpose: the line under the tiles, “Tap a tile to show only those jars”, the tile filter, the Search page and everything a jar stores.",
            "Updated: How it works (Your jars, Search) and the written specification.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "6.3 (build 6)", date: "25 Nov 2026", lines: [
            "New: tap a tile on the Jars list to show only those jars. Before (6.2): the three tiles at the top (Fermenting, In the fridge, Done) only showed the numbers; tapping them did nothing. Now: each tile is a button. Tap one and only that group of jars stays in the list. The tile gets an outline in its own colour, and a line under the tiles says “Showing only: In the fridge” with a button, Show all. Tap the same tile again, or Show all, and every jar is back. With nothing chosen, a small grey line under the tiles says “Tap a tile to show only those jars”. When a group has no jars, the list says so, for example “No jars in the fridge.” The choice is not kept: when you close the app and open it again, all jars are shown. The orange Fermenting tile stays filled as before; filled still only means “first tile of the row”, and the outline is what means “chosen”.",
            "New: see a picture large. Before: pictures could only be looked at small (60 by 76 points on the head card of a jar, 120 by 120 in the row of pictures); tapping them did nothing. Now: tap the picture on a jar’s head card, or any picture in the row of pictures on a jar, a recipe or a bake page, and it opens large on a dark page. The picture fills the screen as far as it fits. At the top it says “2 of 3” and there is a Done button. On the iPhone and iPad, swipe left and right to go through the pictures of that jar (or recipe, or bake). On the Mac, the buttons Previous and Next (and the left and right arrow keys) go through them, and Escape closes the page. A small grey line under the row of pictures says “Tap a picture to see it large”. The pages where you change something (with Remove under each picture) are unchanged: there a tap on a picture still does nothing.",
            "Updated: How it works (Your jars, A jar’s page, Recipes, Bakes, Signs in this app) and the written specification.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "6.2 (build 5)", date: "25 Nov 2026", lines: [
            "Changed: the three numbers at the top of the Jars list. Before: one line of small words, “2 fermenting · 1 in the fridge · 1 done”, each number in its colour. Now: three small tiles side by side, each with a big number and its name under it in small capitals. FERMENTING is an orange tile filled with orange, with white words. IN THE FRIDGE is a pale blue tile with a blue number. DONE is a pale green tile with a green number. The colours are the same as the pills on each jar (orange Fermenting, blue In fridge, green Done). Under the tiles nothing changes: the button Start a jar, and the box “1 jar is due for a taste test”.",
            "New: the jar’s own picture on its head card. Before: the orange card at the top of a jar’s page always showed a drawn jar, even when the jar had pictures. Now: when the jar has at least one picture, its first picture stands there instead, in a rounded frame. A jar with no pictures still shows the drawn jar. The row of pictures under the card is unchanged and shows all of them. The Jars list already showed the first picture as each row’s small picture; now the jar’s page matches it.",
            "Updated: How it works (Your jars, A jar’s page, Signs in this app) and the written specification.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "6.1 (build 4)", date: "25 Nov 2026", lines: [
            "Changed: how a jar’s day is counted wherever the app says “Day N”. Before: the day you started a jar was Day 0, so a jar started today said “Day 0” (and “DAY 0” in the Timeline), and the next day said Day 1. Now: the start day is Day 1, the next day is Day 2, and so on. Example: a jar started on Mon 23 Nov says Day 3 on Wed 25 Nov (before: Day 2). It shows in four places: the big “Day N” on the jar’s head card; the pill in the jar list (“Fermenting · day 3”); the day headings of the Timeline (“DAY 1  WED 25 NOV”); and the words of the two notifications (“Morning burp · day 3” and “Day 3 — time to taste and write a line”). For a jar in the fridge, or done, the number still stops on the day it went into the fridge (now one higher than before).",
            "Unchanged on purpose: “days fermented” as a length of time. Insights (the chart “Rating and days fermented” and “Days: about 12” in the recommended recipe) and the column “Days fermented” in the spreadsheet still count whole days gone by: started on Monday and into the fridge the next Monday is 7 days. Also unchanged: when a taste test is due (still counted from the last taste test, or from the start day).",
            "Fixed: on the iPhone, in Insights, the headings “Recommended recipe” and “Rating and brine strength” broke into two lines because the small note stood beside them. Now: the note stands under the heading when both do not fit on one line. On iPad and Mac it stays beside the heading.",
            "Updated: How it works (the jar page and Signs in this app now say the start day is Day 1) and the written specification (the day-number rule).",
        ]),
        Release(version: "6.1 (build 3)", date: "25 Nov 2026", lines: [
            "New: a new look, “Full colour”. Nothing about what FermentLog stores or does changes; only how the pages look. Before (6.0): plain lists of facts on flat white boxes on a beige page. Now: the pages are built from colour, cards and small tiles. You picked this look from a picture.",
            "New: a head card on every read page. A jar, a recipe, a bake and Insights now open with a card filled with the colour of its part (Jars orange, Bakes gold-brown, Recipes purple, Insights teal) and white words. Before: the page began with a plain list of facts.",
            "Changed: the page behind the cards. Before: flat white boxes on beige. Now: the page is lightly tinted with the colour of the part, and the cards have rounder corners and a soft shadow.",
            "Changed: facts sit in small tiles. Before: one line per fact (“Lid  Plain lid”), eight lines under each other. Now: each fact is a small tile, with its name in small capitals and the value right under it.",
            "Changed: days are written in plain words. Before: 2026-11-25. Now: “Wed 25 Nov” (with the year only when it is not this year). The pages where you change something, and the files you export, keep their date pickers and the old way of writing the day.",
            "Changed: the switches Ferments | Bakes (Insights), What I did | Taste test (Timeline) and Jars | Bakes. Before: drawn in the Mac’s own accent colour (yellow on your Mac). Now: drawn in the colour of their part.",
            "New: the jar page head card. A drawn jar, the jar’s name, a big “Day 2” (the whole days it has been fermenting, counted as before: the start day is day 0), its state (Fermenting, In the fridge or Done) and “started Wed 25 Nov”. Under them a slim line fills from the start day to the next taste test, with “Taste test Sat 28 Nov · in 3 days” under it. Before: a small status pill and the words “Taste test due 2026-11-28”.",
            "Changed: the jar’s facts. Before: eight lines, one fact each. Now: small tiles, three side by side: Vegetable, Form, Brine, Room, Lid, Weight and Logged by. Only the ones you filled in are shown, so an empty fact no longer takes a line with a dash.",
            "Changed: the Burp block. Before: three rows. Now: one row with the Burped button, when the jar was last burped, and the Burp alarm switch with its two times beside it.",
            "Changed: the Timeline is now a rail. Before: every line repeated its date and time and carried a tag, “Did” or “Taste”. Now: each day has ONE heading (“DAY 1  THU 26 NOV”, the day counted from the start day, which is day 0); beside the word Timeline stands “10 lines · tap a line to change it”; when several lines share a time, the time is written once; every line has a dot on a thin vertical line. A filled orange dot means something you did. A green ring with the word “Taste” means a taste test. Tapping a line still opens “Change this line”.",
            "Changed: adding a Timeline line. Before: five rows. Now: two rows: the switch and “When”, and the text box with Add right beside it.",
            "Changed: Insights starts with four number tiles. Before: four text lines in a box called “Your jars”. Now: Jars, Active now, Success (with a small ring that shows the share) and Average rating, each in its own tile.",
            "Changed: the two labels “Show” in Insights are gone. Before: a label and a switch, then a label and a vegetable list, each on its own row. Now: the Ferments | Bakes switch and the vegetable chooser sit on one row.",
            "Changed: Recommended recipe and Best jar so far. Before: text lines and a text link. Now: Recommended recipe is a head card with five tiles (Vegetable, Brine, Room, Days, Lid), and Best jar so far has a button, “Open this jar”.",
            "Changed: the dot charts (rating and brine strength, room temperature, days fermented). Before: small dots on a dashed grid. Now: larger dots with a white edge, faint lines only for the stars 1 to 5, and “each dot is one jar” beside the heading. The brine chart shades the usual range, 2 to 3 %.",
            "Changed: “Average rating by vegetable / form / lid” and “Problems that came up”. Before: thick square bars with grid lines and an axis under every chart. Now: one slim round bar per line, with the figure right beside the name (“Cabbage 4.0 ★”). Bake insights follows the same pattern: number tiles and slim bars.",
            "New: the recipe page head card. Name, category, description, two tiles “Hands-on” and “Start to finish”, and the buttons in one row (the main one white on the colour). Before: a plain page with the category pill and a column of buttons.",
            "Changed: Ingredients. Before: one line each, amount and words run together. Now: the amount stands in its own column at the left (“100 g” · “active sourdough starter”). On a wide window Ingredients and Equipment sit side by side.",
            "New: a time ribbon above the Method, with one numbered piece per step, as long as the step takes. A pale piece is a wait of 2 hours or more; a full-colour piece is a shorter step. It shows only when the steps have times written. Below it the steps are numbered circles on a thin line, each with its time in a small tag. Before: a numbered list with the time as a tag.",
            "Changed: the bake page. Before: pills, then a plain block of stars. Now: a head card with the bake’s name, category, day and recipe, and the ratings (Crust, Crumb, Flavour, Overall) as slim bars with the stars beside the name.",
            "Unchanged on purpose: everything FermentLog keeps and does: your jars, bakes, recipes and lessons, the alarms, iCloud, safety copies, export and the spreadsheet files.",
            "Updated: the written specification (docs/spec), How it works (the jar page, Timeline, Insights, recipe page and bake page, and Signs in this app) and this list.",
        ]),
        Release(version: "6.0 (build 2)", date: "25 Nov 2026", lines: [
            "Fixed: moving recipe steps on the Mac. Before (build 1): the page New recipe / Edit recipe said “Drag a step to move it”, but on the Mac dragging did nothing, so the order of the steps could not be changed there at all. Now: every step on the Mac has a small drag handle (three short lines) at its right end. Hold the handle, drag the step onto another step and let go: it takes that step’s place. The step you are holding it over turns light purple, so you see where it will land. iPhone and iPad are unchanged.",
            "Changed: the Save button on the Mac. Before: on the pages New jar / Edit jar, Log a bake / Edit bake and New recipe / Edit recipe, Save at the top right was a plain bold word in a pale pill, just like Cancel. Now: it is in the full colour of its part (orange for jars, gold-brown for bakes, purple for recipes), so you see at once which one saves.",
            "Fixed: the Mac’s own Settings window (the one ⌘, opens). Before: it always followed the Mac’s own look, even when you had set Settings › Appearance to Light or Dark. Now: it follows Appearance (System, Light or Dark) like the rest of the app.",
            "Changed: the page Log a bake / Edit bake. Before: the headings Photos, How it turned out, Problems and Notes were black and “Delete this bake” was grey. Now: the headings are in the Bakes colour (gold-brown) and “Delete this bake” is red, like on the jar and recipe pages.",
            "Fixed: the chart “Rating trend, oldest to newest” in Bake insights. Before: the line along the bottom showed halves such as “0,5” and “1,5”, although a bake is always a whole one. Now: it counts whole bakes: 1, 2, 3 …",
        ]),
        Release(version: "6.0 (build 1)", date: "25 Nov 2026", lines: [
            // The big change
            "New: FermentLog is now a real app for iPhone, iPad and Mac. Before (web app 5.25): a web page you put on the iPhone’s Home Screen, one copy per browser. Now: one app from TestFlight on each device, with its own icon, its own notifications and, on the Mac, its menus and keys (⌘N new jar, ⌥⌘N new bake, ⇧⌘N new recipe, ⌘F search, ⌘, Settings).",
            "Note: “Bring your jars over from the web app” needs the web app at version 5.26 or newer on the iPhone. Older copies could not save a backup file from the Home Screen, so open the web app, let it update, and then make the backup.",
            "New: iCloud keeps your iPhone, iPad and Mac on the same jars. Before: every device (and every browser) had its own jars, and moving them meant a backup file. Now: one file in iCloud Drive, in the folder FermentLog, holds all your jars, bakes, recipes, lessons, templates, people and lists, with the pictures beside it. A change on one device shows up on the others by itself. Settings › iCloud shows the state, the time of the last sync and a Sync now button.",
            "New: when two devices change things at the same time, nothing is lost. Each jar, bake, recipe and lesson is joined one by one and the newest change to each one wins. Things you delete stay hidden for 30 days, then leave for good. Before: there was only one copy, so this could not happen.",
            "New: “Update this device first”. If one device has a newer FermentLog that stores more details, a device still on the older one shows a red bar with these words. It saves nothing, makes no safety copies and cannot import or restore, so it can never save your file without the new details. Update it in TestFlight and the bar goes away. Nothing is lost meanwhile.",
            // Burp
            "New: the “Burped” button. A fermenting jar’s page has a block “Burp” with a full-colour Burped button. One tap writes “Burped the jar” and the time into the Timeline and shows “Last burp: today 08:12 · 2 today” beside the button, with a bar at the bottom and Undo for 4 seconds. Before: you typed “burped the jar” as a step by hand.",
            "New: the burp alarm. FermentLog rings twice a day, at 08:00 in the morning and 20:00 in the evening (change both in Settings › Burp alarm), for every fermenting jar whose alarm is on: “Burp: Cabbage jar — Morning burp · day 3. Tap Burped when it is done.” The notification has its own Burped button that writes the burp into the Timeline without opening the app; tapping the notification itself opens the jar. Before: nothing rang.",
            "New: the alarm is on by itself for jars with a plain lid and off for the other three lids (airlock, burping / self-venting, cloth / open). Each jar has its own “Burp alarm” switch, on its page and in its form, with a line under it that says why (“On by itself for a plain lid”, “Off: this lid lets the gas out by itself”, “On for this jar”, “Off for this jar”). Each device has its own switch too, “Ring on this iPhone / iPad / Mac” in Settings › Burp alarm: on from the start on the iPhone, off on the iPad and the Mac.",
            "New: the alarm stops by itself. It rings only for jars that are still fermenting: moving a jar to the fridge, recording its outcome or deleting it ends it at once. A ring is skipped when you logged a burp with the Burped button in the 3 hours before it, and a notification still on your screen goes away when you burp the jar. It plans 7 days ahead (up to 14 rings per jar, 60 in all) and renews the plan every time you open the app.",
            "New: the jar list shows a small grey tag “Burp alarm on” on every jar the alarm rings for.",
            "New: FermentLog asks for permission to notify by itself, once, the first time something should ring on this device (for example a plain-lid jar that is fermenting, on the iPhone). Say Allow and the alarm works. Settings › Burp alarm shows the state: a green tick and “Notifications are allowed”, or, if you said no, a red line with an Open Settings button; the button “Allow notifications” is there too.",
            // Taste reminder
            "Changed: the taste-test reminder is now a real notification. Before (5.25): it showed only while you had the web app open, as an orange bar and a clock mark. Now: on the day a taste test is due the device rings at the morning time (08:00 unless you changed it): “Taste test: Cabbage jar — Day 3 — time to taste and write a line.” Settings › Taste-test reminder has a switch for each device (on from the start on the iPhone, off on iPad and Mac). The list still shows the tinted box “2 jars are due for a taste test”, the clock mark is now the small tag “Taste test due”, and the jar’s page says “Taste test due 2026-11-28”.",
            "Unchanged on purpose: the days are counted from your last Taste line (or the start date), and only a Taste line moves the next reminder; a Did line such as “Burped the jar” does not. Each jar has its own number of days (0 to 60, 3 to begin with, 0 = off), set in the jar form.",
            // Recipes
            "New: a To-do list on a jar started from a recipe. “Start a jar from this recipe” now gives the jar a block “To do — from <recipe>” with the recipe’s steps as round boxes and “2 of 7 done” above them. Tick a step and it turns green, shows “done 14:05”, and a Did line with the step’s words and the time is written into the Timeline. Tick it again to take the tick and that line back. Before: the steps were not carried over as a list.",
            "New: “Save steps as a recipe” on a jar’s page. It opens a new recipe already filled in: title = the jar’s name, category Vegetable ferment, ingredients (vegetable and cut, grams of salt, ml of water, spices), equipment (vessel, weight, lid) and one step for each Did line in time order (burps are left out). Nothing is saved until you press Save. The button shows only when the jar has at least one Did line besides burps.",
            "Changed: the recipe buttons. Before: “Start a batch from this” and “Log a bake from this”. Now: “Start a jar from this recipe” (on every recipe except Sourdough ones) and “Log a bake from this recipe” (on every recipe except Vegetable ferment ones; the full-colour button on a Sourdough recipe). The word “batch” is gone from the screens; it is “jar” everywhere.",
            // Jar page and form
            "Changed: the Timeline on a jar. Before: a ✕ at the end of each line. Now: tap a line to change its day, time or words in “Change this line” (Cancel and Save), or press “Delete this line”, which asks “Delete this line?” with “Keep it”. Adding is as before: the switch “What I did” / “Taste test”, a day-and-time box, one step per line (paste a whole list and the list numbers go away) and a full-colour Add button.",
            "Changed: words instead of crosses. Before: ✕ took things away (a line, a photo, a recipe step, a lesson, a person). Now: the word “Remove” or “Delete” in red, and after a jar, recipe, bake, lesson or template is removed a bar at the bottom offers Undo for 4 seconds. A jar removed by mistake can still be brought back from a safety copy for 30 days.",
            "Changed: no more emoji. Before: the web app used emoji for the fridge, the AI helper, search, backup and more. Now: small hand-drawn marks (jar, recipe book, plus, chart, gear, bread, search) and words on the buttons: “Move to the fridge”, “Build jar with AI”, “Search”.",
            "Changed: each part has its own colour. Before: orange almost everywhere. Now: Jars orange, Bakes gold-brown, Recipes purple, Insights teal, Search slate blue, Settings grey-blue, each with its own headings, marks and main button. A main button is always full colour, never grey. Day and night colours are set for each.",
            "Changed: Share does the job of Print. Before: Print on a jar and on a recipe. Now: Share sends the same plain-words summary (a jar: name, status, brine, temperature, equipment, dates, result, notes and the whole Timeline) through the system’s share sheet, which also offers a printer. There is no Print button any more.",
            "Changed: Move to the fridge. Before: “🧊 Move to fridge”. Now: “Move to the fridge”, one tap on a fermenting jar’s page. It stops the burp alarm and the taste-test reminder.",
            "Changed: the jar list. Before: one list with an orange banner and a clock mark when a taste test was due. Now: the numbers “2 fermenting · 1 in the fridge · 1 done”, then three groups (Fermenting, In the fridge, Done), each with its count. Fermenting jars that are due for a taste test come first, the most overdue first. Each row has the picture or a jar mark, the name, the status pill with the day number, the brine, the stars and the tags.",
            // Safety, backups
            "Changed: a safety copy every day. Before (5.25): a note “Time for a backup” appeared on Home when your chosen interval (weekly, every 2 weeks, monthly, every 3 months or off; monthly to begin with) had passed. Now: the first time anything is saved each day, FermentLog writes “Daily <date>” into the Safety copies folder next to your file, checks it by reading it back, and keeps the newest 30. The pictures are copied beside them and never removed. Settings › iCloud › Safety copies lists them newest first with “4 jars · 2 bakes · 2 recipes”, and “Restore this copy” brings one back (a copy of how things are now is kept first, called “Before restore”). “Make a safety copy now” makes one that is kept for good.",
            "Removed: the backup reminder and its interval setting. It is no longer needed, because the daily copies and iCloud do that work.",
            "Unchanged: Export is still in Settings. Spreadsheet (.xlsx, four tabs: Ferments, Bakes, Recipes, Lessons), CSV (one file, four labelled parts) and Backup file (a .json file with all jars, bakes, recipes, templates, lessons and settings, but not the pictures). New: “Restore from a backup file” in the same place asks first, makes a safety copy and joins the file with what is here. Before: “Export backup (JSON)”, “Import backup” and “Export spreadsheet (Excel)”.",
            "New: “Bring your jars over from the web app” in Settings, in three steps: open the old FermentLog on the iPhone, Settings › Backup & export › Export backup (JSON), save the file to Files, then here choose that file. The app shows what is inside (“7 jars, 2 bakes, 3 recipes …”), makes a safety copy “Before import”, adds everything and deletes nothing. Doing it twice with the same file adds nothing twice. The pictures come across, and so do your people and your own vegetables, cuts and spices, which the old backup did not hold, so FermentLog works them out from your entries.",
            // AI
            "Changed: the AI key. Before: kept in the browser of that one device. Now: kept in the keychain of this device only. It does not travel through iCloud and is in no backup or copy, so enter it once on each device in Settings › AI assistant. Everything else about the AI is as before: your own key, only the words you write are sent, and you check the result before anything is saved.",
            // What differs
            "Changed: the lessons notebook shows the day a lesson was written. Before: the day and the time. The form keeps text, who logged it, the recipe and the list “From a jar or bake”.",
            "Changed: Settings. Before: People, Backup & export, Backup reminder, AI assistant, Appearance, Manage vegetables & spices, What’s new and install help. Now: Who is logging, Burp alarm, Taste-test reminder, Appearance (System, Light or Dark, for this device), iCloud, Bring your jars over from the web app, Export, AI assistant (optional) and, at the end, Your lists, How it works, What’s new and the version number (6.0 (1)).",
            "Removed: what only a web page needed: “Add to Home Screen”, “fully close and reopen after an update”, and the banner and dot that pointed to What’s new. Updates now come through TestFlight.",
            "New: How it works is written for the whole app, with a part “Signs in this app” for the native marks and colours (the tick boxes of the To-do list, the Undo bar, the drag handle and the rest).",
            "Unchanged on purpose: the brine sum (salt ÷ (salt + water) × 100; 2 to 3 % is the sweet spot, only a hint), the status rules (Fermenting, In fridge, Done), ratings and Insights (best jar, a recommended recipe from two jars rated 4 or more), Search, bakes, recipes, the sourdough example, who is logging, the spreadsheet columns. Dates and times now follow the device you hold (the web app used universal time in a few places).",
            "Updated: the written specification (docs/spec) and this guide.",
        ]),
    ]

    struct Section: Identifiable {
        let title: String
        let text: String
        var id: String { title }
    }

    static let howItWorks: [Section] = [
        Section(title: "The basics", text: """
FermentLog is your private logbook for fermenting jars, baking and recipes. It is an app for iPhone, iPad and Mac, and all three show the same jars (see “iPhone, iPad and Mac together”). It works without internet; iCloud only passes your data between your own devices.
On the iPhone there are five tabs along the bottom:
• Home — your jars. A switch at the top, Jars | Bakes, shows your bakes instead. The Search button is at the top right.
• Recipes — your recipe book.
• New — three big buttons: New jar, New bake, New recipe.
• Insights — what makes your best jars and bakes.
• Settings — alarms, iCloud, safety copies, export, the AI assistant and this guide.
On the iPad and the Mac the same things stand in a list at the side: Jars, Bakes, Recipes, Insights, Search, Settings. A New button at the top right of the page offers New jar, New bake and New recipe. On the Mac these keys work: ⌘N new jar, ⌥⌘N new bake, ⇧⌘N new recipe, ⌘F search, ⌘, Settings; the Help menu opens this guide and What’s new.
Every page where you change something has Cancel and Save; on the Mac, Escape is Cancel, and Save is in the full colour of its part (orange for jars, gold-brown for bakes, purple for recipes). The main button of a page is always in full colour. Each part of the app has its own colour: Jars orange, Bakes gold-brown, Recipes purple, Insights teal, Search slate blue, Settings grey-blue.
Settings › Appearance picks System, Light or Dark for this device only; on the Mac it also decides how the Settings window (⌘,) looks. The version number is at the bottom of Settings.
"""),

        Section(title: "Your jars", text: """
The Jars list (Home on the iPhone, Jars at the side on iPad and Mac) starts with three small tiles side by side, each with a big number: FERMENTING (the orange tile), IN THE FRIDGE (the pale blue tile) and DONE (the pale green tile). The colours are the colours of the pills on the rows below. The tiles are buttons. Tap one to show only those jars: the tile gets an outline in its own colour and a line under the tiles says “Showing only: In the fridge” with the button Show all. Tap the same tile again, or Show all, and every jar is back. With nothing chosen, a small grey line says “Tap a tile to show only those jars”. If a group has no jars, the list says so (“No jars in the fridge.”). Your choice is not kept: after you close the app, all jars are shown again. Under the tiles is a find box, “Find a jar by name or vegetable”: type a few letters and only the jars whose name or vegetable contains them stay in the list, at once, in all three groups. Big or small letters and accents do not matter (“surkal” finds “Surkål”). The word Clear empties the box. If no jar fits, the list says “No jar matches “xyz”.” The box works together with a tile (In the fridge and “carrot” shows the carrot jars in the fridge). It starts empty every time the list opens. Next to it (under it on a narrow iPhone) a switch, Sort, puts the jars in order: Start date or Name. Your choice is remembered on this device; the iPhone, the iPad and the Mac each keep their own. Then comes the full-colour button Start a jar. On the iPad and the Mac there is also New jar at the top right.
If any jar is due for a taste test, a tinted box says “2 jars are due for a taste test” and names them.
Jars are in three groups, each with its count:
• Fermenting — on the counter. With Sort on Start date: jars due for a taste test come first, the most overdue first; the others follow, newest first.
• In the fridge — newest first (Start date).
• Done — a verdict has been recorded; newest first (Start date).
With Sort on Name, every group is in A to Z order, the way your device’s language sorts (in Swedish Å, Ä and Ö come after Z), and the taste-test order does not apply; the box “jars are due for a taste test” stays on top either way.
Each row shows the jar’s newest Timeline picture (else its own first picture, else a jar mark), its name (the vegetable when it has no name), a status pill with the day number (“Fermenting · day 3”; the day you started the jar is day 1), the brine percentage and, once rated, its stars. Two small tags can follow: “Taste test due” and “Burp alarm on”. Tap a row to open the jar.
With no jars yet you see a big jar mark, “No jars yet” and Start a jar.
"""),

        Section(title: "Starting a jar", text: """
Tap Start a jar (or New jar). The vegetable is the only thing you must have; Save stays off until there is one. Carrot and Sticks are chosen to begin with, so change them. The page has these parts, in order:
• Start — only when you have saved templates or an AI key. “Start from a template” fills in the conditions, spices and equipment of a template; your name, date, photos and notes stay. With an AI key you can also describe the jar in your own words and press Build jar with AI; look it over, then Save.
• Basics — Name (for example Spicy carrot #1), Vegetable and Form (each a list that ends in “Add your own…”; Form can be None), Start date (today to begin with) and Logged by (only when you added people in Settings; a new jar starts with whoever is logging).
• Brine and conditions — Salt (g) 25 and Water (ml) 1000 to begin with, Room temperature (°C) 20, Jar size (ml) 1000. A line under the salt and water works out the brine for you: salt ÷ (salt + water). From 2 % to 3 % it reads “Brine 2.4 % ✓ in the 2–3 % sweet spot” in green; outside that it says “typical range is 2–3 %”, which is only a hint, not an error.
• Spices — tap the ones you use: Garlic, Dill, Mustard seed, Black pepper, Bay leaf, Ginger, Chilli, and your own. “Add your own spice” and Add put a new one in the list for next time.
• Equipment — Vessel (type your own words), Weight (Glass weight, Ceramic weight, Cabbage leaf, Smaller jar, None) and Lid (Airlock, Burping / self-venting lid, Plain lid, Cloth / open).
• Reminders — “Remind me to taste every 3 days” (0 to 60; 0 turns the taste-test reminder off for this jar). Under it the switch Burp alarm. It follows the lid until you touch it: the small line under it says “On by itself for a plain lid” or “Off: this lid lets the gas out by itself”. Once you flip it yourself it stays as you set it (“On for this jar” or “Off for this jar”), even if you change the lid later.
• Photos and notes — Add pictures, Take a picture (iPhone and iPad) and a notes box. Pictures are shrunk to 1600 pixels on the long side.
At the bottom: Save as a template asks for a name (for example “Classic 2.5% carrots”) and keeps the vegetable, cut, salt, water, temperature, jar size, spices, vessel, weight, lid and taste-test days, so the next jar can start from it. When you change an existing jar, Delete this jar asks first and then shows Undo for 4 seconds.
After Save, a new jar opens on its own page.
"""),

        Section(title: "A jar’s page", text: """
At the top right: Edit. Then, from the top:
• An orange head card: the newest picture of the Timeline in a rounded frame, so it shows how the jar looks now (when the Timeline has no picture, the jar’s own first picture; a drawn jar when there is none; tap the picture to see it large: from the Timeline it opens the photo story at that picture, otherwise the jar’s own pictures), the jar’s name, a big “Day 3” (Day 1 is the day you started the jar, Day 2 the next day, and so on), its state (Fermenting, In the fridge or Done), “started Wed 25 Nov”, and, while a taste test is coming, a slim line that fills from the start day to the next taste test with “Taste test Sat 28 Nov · in 3 days” under it (deep orange once the day has come).
• Pictures (tap one to see it large on a dark page, with “2 of 3” and Done at the top; on the iPhone and iPad swipe left and right to go through them, on the Mac use Previous and Next or the arrow keys, and Escape closes it; a small grey line under the row says “Tap a picture to see it large”), then six tiles: Vegetable, Form, Brine (green when it is 2–3 %), Room, Lid and Weight, plus Logged by when set; your spices as small tags, and your notes.
• Burp (only while the jar is fermenting) — all on one row: the full-colour Burped button; beside it “Not burped yet” or “Last burp: today 08:12 · 2 today”; and the switch Burp alarm with its two times (“Rings at 08:00 and 20:00”) beside it (with “— switched off for this device in Settings” when this device does not ring). One tap on Burped writes the burp into the Timeline and plays a random burp from the ones switched on in Settings › Burp sounds (none on a silent iPhone); a bar at the bottom says “Burp logged 08:12” with Undo for 4 seconds.
• To do — from <recipe> (only when the jar was started from a recipe) — the recipe’s steps as round boxes, with “2 of 7 done” above them. Tap a box: it turns green with a tick, the step shows “done 14:05”, and a Did line with the step’s words and that time is written into the Timeline. Tap it again to take both back (if you have changed that Timeline line’s words or time in between, the line stays).
• Timeline — everything about the jar, oldest first, drawn as a rail. Each day has one heading (“DAY 1 · WED 25 NOV”; Day 1 is the day you started the jar); the time is written once when several lines share it; every line has a dot on a thin vertical line. A filled orange dot is something you did, such as chopping the cabbage or burping the jar. A green ring with the word “Taste” is a taste test. Tap a line to change its day, its time (“Has a time” can be switched off) or its words, then Save or Cancel; “Delete this line” asks first. A line can carry pictures: they show as small squares under its words, and a line with only pictures says “Picture” (What I did) or “Taste” alone. Tap a picture to open the jar’s photo story: every Timeline picture of the jar, oldest first, opened at the one you tapped, with “3 of 8” and Done at the top (swipe left and right on the iPhone and iPad; Previous, Next and the arrow keys on the Mac; Escape closes it) and a caption at the bottom such as “Day 3 · Wed 25 Nov 08:12 — Taste: tangy, still crunchy”. In Change this line a section Pictures shows the line’s pictures (Remove under each), Add pictures and Take a picture (iPhone and iPad); Save works with words or pictures. To add a line, pick What I did or Taste test and set When (now to begin with; change it if it was earlier), write in the box, then pick pictures if you like (Add pictures for up to 6 from Photos, Take a picture on iPhone and iPad; each shows as a small square with Remove under it) and press the full-colour Add. Add works with words, pictures or both; a picture alone is a line too. When you paste several lines in What I did, the pictures go with the last one. Share and the spreadsheet write “(1 picture)” after a line’s words; the pictures themselves are not in them. In What I did every line you type or paste becomes its own step, and list numbers (“1.”, “2)”) and bullets are taken off; “2% salt” stays. In Taste test the whole text is one note, and it moves the next taste-test reminder. With an AI key, Taste test also offers “Add by voice”: write or dictate “day 4, tangy and still crunchy” and it makes the line.
• Move to the fridge (fermenting jars only) — one tap. Today is written as the fridge day, the status becomes In fridge and the alarms stop. There is no way back from the jar’s page, so tap it when you mean it.
• Outcome — “No verdict recorded yet” and Record outcome; or the verdict in a green box “Success” or a red box “Didn’t work out”, with Taste, Sourness, Crunch and Overall as stars and the problems as red tags (Mould, Kahm yeast, Mushy / soft, Too salty, Not sour enough, Off smell). Edit outcome changes it. Recording an outcome makes the jar Done.
• Duplicate (same set-up, a fresh jar called “… (copy)” with no lines, pictures or verdict; it opens at once), Share (a plain-words summary through the system’s share sheet) and Save steps as a recipe (it opens a new recipe filled in from the jar: ingredients, equipment and one step for each Did line, burps left out; nothing is saved until you press Save; the button shows only when there is a Did line besides burps). With an AI key there is also AI tips, with Add to notes or Dismiss.
"""),

        Section(title: "The burp alarm", text: """
Gas builds up in a closed jar, so some jars need a burp every day. The alarm reminds you twice a day.
• What rings: at 08:00 and 20:00 (change both in Settings › Burp alarm), for every jar that is fermenting and has its alarm on, you get “Burp: Cabbage jar — Morning burp · day 3. Tap Burped when it is done.”
• The Burped button is on the notification itself: press and hold it (or pull it down) and tap Burped. “Burped the jar” and the time are written into the jar’s Timeline without opening the app. Tapping the notification itself opens the jar.
• The burp sound: every Burped plays a burp, both the button on the notification and the Burped button on the jar’s page. It is one of 10 (Classic, Short pop, Chuckle, Long one, Squeaky, Double, Bubbles, Rising, Tiny, Ta-da), all cheerful and bright,, picked at random from the ones that are switched on, never the same one twice in a row. On the notification a small note also shows, “Burped: Cabbage jar — Written in the Timeline at 08:12. Burp: Chuckle.”, and goes away by itself after about 8 seconds. If FermentLog is open on the screen, you only hear the burp. You hear nothing when the iPhone is on silent (the switch on the side) or in a Focus that silences FermentLog; for the notification also when notification sounds for FermentLog are off in the system Settings.
• Burp sounds in Settings: Settings › Burp sounds lists the 10 burps. Play lets you hear one at once (even on silent); the switch puts it in or out of the random mix. All ten are on to begin with. With all off, Burped makes no sound. The choice is kept on each device on its own.
• Which jars: a jar with a plain lid rings by itself. The other lids (airlock, burping / self-venting, cloth / open) do not, because the gas leaves on its own. Every jar has its own switch Burp alarm, on its page and in its form, to choose differently. The jar list marks the jars that ring with the grey tag “Burp alarm on”.
• Which devices: Settings › Burp alarm › “Ring on this iPhone” (or iPad, or Mac). It is on from the start on the iPhone and off on iPad and Mac, because the iPhone is the one in your pocket. It is a choice for each device. The two times are the same on every device.
• When it stays quiet: if you logged a burp with the Burped button (on the page or on a notification) in the 3 hours before a ring, that ring is skipped; a Did line you type yourself does not count. A notification still on your screen goes away when you burp the jar afterwards. The alarm stops for good when the jar goes to the fridge, gets its outcome or is deleted.
• It plans 7 days ahead and renews its plan each time you open the app or change a jar, so open FermentLog at least once a week.
• Allowing notifications: the first time something should ring on a device, FermentLog asks by itself; say Allow. Settings › Burp alarm shows the state: a green tick and “Notifications are allowed”; or, if you said no, a red line and Open Settings, which takes you to the system’s settings; and “Allow notifications” if it was never asked. On the iPad and the Mac, switch “Ring on this …” on too.
"""),

        Section(title: "Taste-test reminder", text: """
Each jar has its own number of days between taste tests, set in the jar form (3 to begin with; 0 turns it off for that jar).
• The days are counted from your last Taste line, or from the start date when there is none. A Did line (even “Burped the jar”) does not move it; writing a Taste line does.
• On the day it is due, the device rings at the same time as the morning burp (08:00 unless you changed it): “Taste test: Cabbage jar — Day 3 — time to taste and write a line.” If that time has already passed when FermentLog makes its plan (for example a jar that is days overdue), there is no notification, but the jar still shows “Taste test due” in the list, in the tinted box above the groups and on its page.
• Only jars that are fermenting get a reminder; one in the fridge or done does not.
• Settings › Taste-test reminder › “Remind on this iPhone” (or iPad, or Mac) switches it for the device: on from the start on the iPhone, off on iPad and Mac. It uses the same permission as the burp alarm (see above).
"""),

        Section(title: "Recipes", text: """
The Recipes tab (Recipes at the side on iPad and Mac) is a recipe book for fermenting and baking, the newest changed first. A row shows the picture (or a book mark), the title, a purple category pill, “Hands-on …”, “Total …” and the number of steps. With no recipes yet you get New recipe and “Add the sourdough example”.
A recipe holds: Title, Category (Sourdough, Vegetable ferment, Drink / kombucha, Dairy, Other), Description, Pictures, Ingredients and Equipment (one per line), Hands-on time, Total time, the Method (each step has a title, a time hint such as “3 min” and what to do; drag a step by its handle to move it (on the Mac, drop it onto another step and it takes that step’s place); Remove takes one away; Add a step adds one) and Storage and keeping. “Delete this recipe” asks first and then offers Undo for 4 seconds.
On a recipe’s page, a purple head card holds the name, category, description, two tiles (Hands-on and Start to finish) and the buttons in one row, the main one white on the colour. Under it, Ingredients show the amount in its own column at the left (“100 g” · “active sourdough starter”), and on a wide window Ingredients and Equipment sit side by side. Above the Method a time ribbon has one numbered piece per step, as long as the step takes: pale for a wait of 2 hours or more, full colour for a shorter step (it shows only when the steps have times). The steps are numbered circles on a thin line, each with its time in a small tag. The recipe’s pictures can be tapped to see them large, in the same way as on a jar (swipe on the iPhone and iPad; Previous, Next and the arrow keys on the Mac; Done or Escape to close). The buttons:
• Start a jar from this recipe — on every recipe except Sourdough. It opens the New jar page already filled in: the name is the recipe’s title, the vegetable is found from the title when it names one (otherwise Carrot stays), spices are ticked when the ingredients mention them, the notes say “From recipe: …”, and the recipe’s steps become the jar’s To do list. Nothing is saved until you press Save. Each tick on the To do list is written into the Timeline with the time.
• Log a bake from this recipe — on every recipe except Vegetable ferment. It opens a new bake linked to the recipe.
• Duplicate, Share and, with an AI key, Improve with AI (it opens the form with the changes for you to check).
• Lessons learned about this recipe are listed at the end.
Save steps as a recipe, on a jar’s page, makes a recipe the other way round.
"""),

        Section(title: "Bakes and lessons", text: """
Bakes are for sourdough and other baking. On the iPhone choose Bakes in the switch at the top of Home; on the iPad and Mac it is Bakes at the side. The full-colour button is Log a bake. A bake’s row shows a picture or a bread mark, its name, a gold-brown category pill, the date, its recipe, its stars (or “Not rated yet”) and its problems as red tags.
• A bake has: Name, Category (Sourdough, Bread, Other), Date, Recipe (None or one of yours), Logged by, Photos, Crust, Crumb, Flavour and Overall (tap a star; tap the same star again to clear it), Problems (Dense crumb, Gummy, Flat / didn’t rise, Over-proofed, Under-proofed, Burnt, Pale crust) and Notes. With an AI key you can describe a bake and press Fill this in. Delete this bake asks first.
• A bake’s page: a gold-brown head card with the name, category, day and recipe; the bake’s pictures can be tapped to see them large, in the same way as on a jar; the ratings (Crust, Crumb, Flavour, Overall) as slim bars with the stars beside the name; problems, notes, who logged it, Duplicate (a copy dated today), Share, a link to its recipe, and “Lessons from this bake” with a box to add one.
• Bake insights and lessons (a button under the bakes, or Insights › Bakes): the numbers (bakes, rated, average rating); the best bake; a trend chart and averages by recipe and by category (they appear once two bakes are rated); how often each problem came up; and the notebook “Lessons learned and improvements”. A lesson has its words, who noticed it, the recipe it is about, the jar or bake it came from and the day it was written. Remove takes one away and offers Undo for 4 seconds. A lesson is tied to a bake when you add it on that bake’s page; the notebook’s own form has the list “From a jar or bake” to tie a lesson to either.
"""),

        Section(title: "Insights", text: """
The Insights tab has a switch, Ferments | Bakes.
Ferments:
• When your jars hold more than one vegetable, a list picks All vegetables or one vegetable, and everything below follows it.
• Four number tiles: Jars, Active now (fermenting or in the fridge), Success (with a small ring that shows the share of finished jars that were a success) and Average rating. The Ferments | Bakes switch and the vegetable chooser sit on one row.
• Recommended recipe: once two finished jars are rated 4 or more, a head card with five tiles, the typical values of those jars (Vegetable, Brine, Room, Days, Lid). Until then it says how to get one.
• With at least two finished, rated jars: Best jar so far (with “Open this jar”), dot charts of the rating against brine strength, room temperature and days fermented (each dot is one jar; the brine chart shades the usual range, 2–3 %), “Rating over your attempts”, the average rating by vegetable, by form and by lid, and “Problems that came up” (one slim bar per line, with the figure beside the name). With fewer, it says “Not enough yet — finish and rate at least two jars and your charts appear here.”
Bakes: number tiles and slim bars in the same way; see “Bakes and lessons”.
Days on read pages are written “Wed 25 Nov” (with the year only when it is not this year).
"""),

        Section(title: "Search", text: """
On the iPhone: Home › Search at the top right. On iPad and Mac: Search at the side (⌘F on the Mac). Results come as you type, grouped as Jars, Bakes and Recipes, each with its count. Every word you type must be found (in any order; capitals do not matter). The find box on the Jars list is smaller: it looks only at the names and vegetables of jars, while Search looks everywhere.
FermentLog looks in:
• Jars — name, vegetable, form, vessel, lid, weight, notes, spices, taste notes, your Did lines and the titles of To do steps.
• Bakes — name, category, recipe name, notes and problems.
• Recipes — title, category, description, storage, ingredients, equipment and the title and words of each step.
It does not look in pictures, in lessons or in the problems of a jar’s outcome.
"""),

        Section(title: "iPhone, iPad and Mac together", text: """
All your data is one file, fermentlog.json, in iCloud Drive (folder FermentLog, which you can also see in Files), with a Photos folder and a Safety copies folder beside it. Use the same Apple ID and keep iCloud Drive on for FermentLog on every device. Settings › iCloud tells you: “In iCloud — the same jars on your iPhone, iPad and Mac” or “Only on this device — iCloud Drive is off”, the State (Up to date, Uploading…, Downloading…), the time of the last sync, and Sync now. FermentLog also looks for news each time you come back to the app and whenever another device saves.
• How it joins: each time a device saves, it first reads what is in the file and joins it with its own changes, jar by jar and recipe by recipe. The newest change to each one wins and nothing the other device wrote is lost. If iCloud sets one write aside because two devices wrote at once, FermentLog joins it in and Settings says “1 clash between devices joined together since this app opened. Nothing was lost.”
• Deleting: what you delete stays hidden for 30 days (so the other devices learn of it), then leaves for good, together with pictures nobody uses any more.
• What is shared: jars, bakes, recipes, lessons, templates, people, your own vegetables, cuts and spices, and the two alarm times. What stays on each device: Appearance, “Ring on this …” and “Remind on this …”, the view you last chose (Jars or Bakes, Ferments or Bakes, What I did or Taste test) and the AI key.
• Update this device first: if another device has a newer FermentLog that stores more details, an older device shows a red bar “Update this device first”, and Settings says so too. It saves nothing, makes no safety copies and cannot import or restore until you update it in TestFlight. Nothing is lost meanwhile.
• Safety copies: the first time anything is saved each day, FermentLog writes “Daily <date>” into the Safety copies folder, checks it by reading it back, and keeps the newest 30. Copies it makes before an import or a restore (“Before import”, “Before restore”) and the ones you make with Make a safety copy now are kept for good. The pictures are copied beside them and never removed. Settings › iCloud › Safety copies lists them, newest first (“4 jars · 2 bakes · 2 recipes”). To go back: Restore this copy, then Restore this copy again to confirm. FermentLog first keeps a copy of how things are now, called “Before restore”; then everything is as it was in the copy, pictures included.
• If saving fails (iCloud not reachable, device full), a bar says “Could not save. Your changes are not stored yet.” and a red line stays in Settings › iCloud until a save works.
"""),

        Section(title: "Bringing your jars over from the web app", text: """
Your jars from the old web app come across in three steps, once:
1. Open the old FermentLog (the web app) on the iPhone.
2. Settings › Backup & export › Export backup (JSON), and save the file to Files.
3. Come back here: Settings › Bring your jars over from the web app › Choose the backup file, and pick that file.
FermentLog shows what is inside (“7 jars, 2 bakes, 3 recipes, 4 lessons, 1 template, 12 pictures”) and asks “Bring in this backup?”. Bring them in makes a safety copy “Before import” first and then adds everything. Nothing is deleted and anything already here stays; if the same jar exists on both sides, the newest change wins. Doing it again with the same file adds nothing twice. The pictures come across too. The old backup did not hold your people or your own vegetables, cuts and spices, so FermentLog collects them from the names on your entries and from your jars. The AI key does not come across; enter it again under Settings › AI assistant.
A backup made by this app (Export › Backup file) is not read here; use “Restore from a backup file” for that.
"""),

        Section(title: "Export and backup files", text: """
Settings › Export:
• Spreadsheet (.xlsx) — four tabs, Ferments, Bakes, Recipes and Lessons; the Did lines of a jar are in the last column of Ferments. It opens in Numbers, Excel and Google Sheets.
• CSV — one file with the same four parts one after the other.
• Backup file — a .json file with all your jars, bakes, recipes, templates, lessons and settings, but not the pictures (they stay in iCloud and in the safety copies).
• Restore from a backup file — asks first, makes a safety copy “Before restore” and joins the file with what is here (it does not replace it). Files from the old web app are for “Bring your jars over” instead.
Files are named fermentlog-<date>. Printing is gone: Share on a jar or a recipe sends a plain-words summary instead.
"""),

        Section(title: "The AI assistant", text: """
Optional. With your own key from Anthropic (console.anthropic.com: make an account, add a little credit, create a key that starts with sk-ant-) you can describe a jar, a recipe or a bake in your own words and let the fields fill in, ask for tips on a jar, improve a recipe, or add a taste note by writing it down. Paste the key under Settings › AI assistant and press Save; “Key saved on this device” appears with a green tick. Remove deletes it at once. You always look over the result before anything is saved.
The key is kept in this device’s keychain only. It does not travel through iCloud and is in no backup, so enter it once on each device. Only the words you write are sent to Anthropic, never your jars, pictures or ratings. You pay Anthropic directly, a fraction of a cent for each use. Without a key the AI buttons are not there, and the rest of FermentLog works as ever.
"""),

        Section(title: "Who is logging", text: """
Settings › Who is logging. Add the names of whoever logs jars, bakes and lessons (the first name you add is chosen at once). Tap a name to log as that person; the pill “Logging” shows who it is now. Jars, bakes and lessons get that name as “Logged by” when you start them, and you can change it on any entry. A new jar, too, starts with that name. Remove asks “Remove <name>?”; their name stays on what they logged. With no people added, the Logged by lists are not shown.
"""),

        Section(title: "Your lists", text: """
Settings › Your lists (near the end of Settings). The vegetables, cuts and spices you added yourself, and your jar templates, each with a line such as “used in 3 jars”.
• Rename changes the name in every jar and template that uses it.
• Remove: a vegetable or a cut can only go when no jar uses it (rename it, or change those jars first); a spice goes and is taken off every jar and template that has it. A template can be removed with Undo for 4 seconds.
The built-in vegetables, cuts and spices are not listed.
"""),

        Section(title: "Signs in this app", text: """
Every mark and colour that means something, and what it tells you.
Pills (round-ended) and tags (small, square-cornered):
• Orange, blue and green mean the state of a jar, on the pills and on the three tiles at the top of the Jars list: orange = Fermenting, blue = In the fridge, green = Done.
• Orange pill “Fermenting · day 3” — the jar is on the counter; the number counts the days since it started, and the start day is Day 1.
• Blue pill “In fridge · day 12” — the jar is in the fridge; the number stops at the day it went in.
• Green pill “Done” — a verdict has been recorded.
• Purple pill — a recipe’s category (for example Sourdough). Gold-brown pill — a bake’s category.
• Grey-blue pill “Logging” — the person whose name goes on new entries.
• Deep orange tag “Taste test due” — this jar needs a taste test now. The same deep orange is used for the tinted box above the groups. On the jar’s own page the head card says it in white: “Taste test due today” or “Taste test due since Sat 28 Nov”.
• On a head card (the coloured card at the top of a jar, a recipe, a bake and the recommended recipe) every word, pill and tile is white, because the card itself carries the colour of the part. The pill still says the same thing: Fermenting, In fridge or Done.
• Grey tag “Burp alarm on” — the burp alarm rings for this jar. Your spices on a jar’s page are grey tags too: a plain fact, nothing to do.
• Red tag — a problem you noted (Mould, Dense crumb …).
Boxes and words:
• Green box “Success” and red box “Didn’t work out” — your verdict on a finished jar.
• Green brine figure — the brine is between 2 and 3 %. A plain figure is outside that range; it is a hint only.
• Red words — something is wrong (“Could not save”, “Notifications are switched off”), or it goes away when you tap it (Delete, Remove). A red bar at the top, “Update this device first”, means this device is a version behind.
• Green “done 14:05” under a To do step — the time you ticked it.
Marks:
• Filled orange dot on the Timeline rail — something you did.
• Green ring with the word “Taste” on the Timeline rail — a taste test.
• A small picture square under a Timeline line — a picture taken with that line. Tap it to see the jar’s photo story, every Timeline picture of the jar, oldest first.
• The slim line on a jar’s head card — how far it is from the last taste test (or the start day) to the next taste test; full means the day has come.
• The green line beside “1 of 3 done” in To do — how many of the steps are ticked.
• A slim bar under a name with stars (a jar’s Outcome, a bake’s How it turned out, the average ratings in Insights) — how many of the five stars; the stars or the figure stand right beside the name. A red slim bar is a problem: how often it came up, the longest bar being the most frequent one.
• A number in a full-colour circle — the number of a recipe step, the same number as its piece in the time ribbon.
• A tile with a coloured outline on the Jars list — the group you chose; only those jars are shown. The line “Showing only: …” and the button Show all say the same, and tapping the tile again brings every jar back.
• A number tile filled with colour (the first tile of a row: Fermenting in the Jars list, Jars in Insights) — it is filled only because it is the first; it means nothing more (a tile is “chosen” only when it has an outline).
• The small ring beside Success in Insights — the share of finished jars that were a success.
• The shaded band in the brine chart — the usual range, 2 to 3 %; the words “shaded = the usual 2–3%” stand beside the chart’s heading.
• The time ribbon above a recipe’s Method — one piece per step, as long as the step takes: a pale piece is a wait of 2 hours or more, a full-colour piece is a shorter step.
• ✓ green tick — in order: the brine is in the sweet spot, notifications are allowed, the AI key is saved.
• Round box with a hand-drawn green tick — a To do step that is done. A grey empty circle is a step still to do.
• ★ ☆ stars — a rating from 1 to 5; a filled star counts and an empty one does not, in the colour of the part. A dash “—” means no rating or no value. “Not rated yet” is a bake with no stars.
• Chips — in Spices and in Problems, a chip with a coloured outline is chosen; a grey chip is not. A tile with a coloured outline on the Jars list means the same: chosen.
• The drag handle at the right of a recipe step (three short lines) — on iPhone, iPad and Mac, hold it and drag to move the step (“Drag a step to move it”). On the Mac you drop the step onto another step and it takes that step’s place; the step you are holding it over turns light purple.
• The Undo bar — a small bar at the bottom after you remove something or log a burp. It goes by itself after 4 seconds; tap Undo before then to take it back.
• Hand-drawn marks — a jar (Jars), a book (Recipes), a loaf (Bakes), a chart (Insights), a magnifying glass (Search), a gear (Settings), a plus (New). Each stands for the part it opens.
• Colours of the parts — orange Jars, gold-brown Bakes, purple Recipes, teal Insights, slate blue Search, grey-blue Settings: headings, marks and the main button of that part.
• A full-colour button — the main thing to do on that page; the softer tinted buttons are the other choices.
"""),
    ]
}
